How Does Communication Happen?

Imagine you send a WhatsApp message:

"Hello"

What happens behind the scenes?

You type "Hello".
Your phone converts it into digital data (bits).
The data travels through your Wi-Fi or mobile network.
It reaches WhatsApp's servers.
The server forwards it to your friend's phone.
Your friend's phone converts the data back into readable text.

Although it feels instant, several networking technologies and protocols work together to make it happen.

Components of a Network

A basic network has five main components:

Sender – The device that sends data (e.g., your laptop).
Receiver – The device that receives data (e.g., a web server).
Transmission Medium – The path the data travels (cable, Wi-Fi, fiber optic, etc.).
Data – The actual information being sent (text, image, video, etc.).
Protocol – The rules that both devices follow to communicate.
Real-Life Analogy: Sending a Letter

Imagine you send a letter to a friend.

Letter System	Computer Network
You	Sender
Friend	Receiver
Road	Transmission Medium
Letter	Data
Postal Rules	Protocol

If either you or the postal service ignores the rules (wrong address, missing stamp), the letter may never arrive. Similarly, devices must follow networking protocols to communicate successfully.

Example: Opening Google

Suppose you open your browser and type:

www.google.com

Behind the scenes:

Your computer connects to the router.
The router forwards the request to your ISP.
A DNS server translates www.google.com into an IP address.
Your request is sent to Google's server.
Google's server processes the request.
The webpage is sent back to your browser.

All of this typically happens in a fraction of a second.

